---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BannerSection from '../../components/BannerSection.astro';
import ResourceVideo from '../../components/ResourceVideo.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'Hiking Conditioning for Colorado: Stretches and Strength That Hold Up on the Trail';
const description =
  'Downhills, not climbs, are what wreck Colorado hikers. The stretches and strength work that prepare knees, hips and ankles for real trails — with the 9NEWS segment.';
const published = '2026-08-11';
const url = `${SITE.url}/resources/hiking-conditioning`;

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why do my knees hurt hiking downhill but not uphill?',
    a: 'Descending is braking. Every downhill step asks your quads and glutes to absorb your bodyweight — more with a pack, more still on steep grades — while lengthening under load. When those muscles fatigue, the braking job shifts to the knee joint itself, and it lets you know. The fix is usually not new knees; it is training the braking muscles the way the trail actually uses them: eccentrically, one leg at a time.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What exercises prepare you for fourteeners?',
    a: 'Three categories cover most of it: step-work for the climb (step-ups onto a box, building toward weighted), eccentric single-leg work for the descent (slow step-downs are gold), and carrying capacity (time under a loaded pack, even on neighborhood walks). Add the calf and hip mobility work and you have covered what the mountain checks. Altitude fitness is its own subject — but legs that are not wrecked help there too.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I stretch before or after hiking?',
    a: 'Before: briefly and dynamically — leg swings, deep squats, ankle circles, two or three minutes at the trailhead. After: this is where longer holds earn their keep, especially calves, hip flexors and quads, which have spent hours working in a short range. The stiffness you feel two days after a big hike is mostly eccentric-load soreness; stretching after helps, but training before helps more.',
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  url,
  datePublished: published,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Jamie Atlas' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': url },
};

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map(item => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:pt-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <p class="font-serif italic text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-3 text-lg">
        <a href="/resources" class="no-underline">Resources</a> · Hiking
      </p>
      <h1 class="mb-8">Hiking conditioning: it's the downhills that get you</h1>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Everyone trains for the climb. Then the summit photo gets taken, the descent starts, and two hours later a
        pair of knees files a formal complaint. <strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">Colorado hiking is a
        braking sport</strong> — and braking is trainable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Site-wide rotating video banner (Jamie, 2026-08-08) -->
  <BannerSection />

  <section class="px-6 py-14 bg-[var(--color-surface)] border-y border-[var(--color-border)]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="mb-6">Watch the segment</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-8">
        I covered hiking stretches and preparation on 9NEWS during my years as their fitness expert — a quick,
        practical primer to pair with the deeper dive below.
      </p>
      <ResourceVideo
        id="NUTTTyyo8qE"
        title="Wellness Wednesday: Stretches and exercises for hiking — 9NEWS"
        caption="From my recurring 9NEWS fitness segments."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-14">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="mb-6">What the trail actually tests</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          <strong>The descent is an eccentric-strength exam.</strong> Going up, muscles shorten as they work —
          hard, but familiar. Coming down, your quads and glutes must produce force while lengthening, step after
          step, often for longer than the climb took. Almost nobody trains this, which is why the soreness after a
          big hike lives in the fronts of the thighs and why knee pain so often starts on the way down.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three things to build in the weeks before big objectives. <strong>Slow step-downs:</strong> stand on a
          step, lower one foot to the floor over three slow seconds, and come back up — the single most
          trail-relevant exercise there is. <strong>Step-ups with intent:</strong> drive through the top leg rather
          than bouncing off the bottom one, adding a pack as it gets easy. <strong>Single-leg balance on uneven
          input:</strong> trails are not flat, and ankles that can react — the same reactive work in the
          <a href="/resources/balance-training">balance training article</a> — are what turn a rolled rock into a
          non-event.
        </p>
        <p>
          And the hips deserve their usual mention: hours of desk sitting shorten the hip flexors, then the trail
          asks for full stride extension a few thousand times. That tug-of-war frequently presents as lower-back
          ache at mile six. It is one of the most fixable patterns I see.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-14 bg-[var(--color-surface)] border-y border-[var(--color-border)]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="mb-6">If a joint already complains on the trail</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          A knee that flares on descents or a hip that tightens by the second hour does not usually mean hiking is
          over — it usually means the load is arriving somewhere untrained. Pain is information, not automatically
          damage. Finding the version of the movement you can do without a lasting flare, then building from it, is
          how hikers get their mileage back. That is the work this studio does every week.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="mb-10">Common questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-8">
        {faq.map(item => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="mb-3">{item.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-20 bg-[var(--color-primary)] text-on-dark">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <h2 class="mb-6 text-white">Want your knees to outlast the trail?</h2>
      <p class="text-xl mb-10 opacity-95">
        A 30-minute consultation — your history, my honest read on whether I can help, and a clear plan if I can.
      </p>
      <a href="/book" class="cta-button cta-button-accent text-lg">Book a consultation</a>
      <p class="mt-8 text-sm opacity-80">
        Or call <a href={`tel:${SITE.contact.phoneTel}`} class="text-white underline">{SITE.contact.phone}</a>
        · <a href="/what-we-treat/knee-pain" class="text-white underline">Read about knee pain</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
